1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,130
A cricket bat hits a ball.

2
00:00:02,130 --> 00:00:03,300
Two marbles click.

3
00:00:03,300 --> 00:00:06,027
A lump of wet clay hits
a wall, and sticks.

4
00:00:06,427 --> 00:00:08,116
Every one is a collision.

5
00:00:08,116 --> 00:00:10,865
But some bounce back, and some just stick.

6
00:00:10,865 --> 00:00:12,113
What is different?

7
00:00:12,562 --> 00:00:14,831
Aarav plays carrom on his study table.

8
00:00:14,831 --> 00:00:17,559
The striker hits a coin,
and both fly apart.

9
00:00:17,909 --> 00:00:20,416
Then he flicks a small
clay ball at a coin.

10
00:00:20,416 --> 00:00:21,402
It just sticks.

11
00:00:21,402 --> 00:00:22,864
Where did the bounce go?

12
00:00:23,364 --> 00:00:26,260
By the end, you will know
what every collision keeps.

13
00:00:26,910 --> 00:00:28,394
So what is a collision?

14
00:00:28,744 --> 00:00:32,402
Two bodies push on each other
hard, for a very short time.

15
00:00:32,402 --> 00:00:33,682
Then they move on.

16
00:00:34,082 --> 00:00:38,430
During the hit, the pushes are equal
and opposite, and outside forces are

17
00:00:38,430 --> 00:00:39,330
tiny.

18
00:00:39,350 --> 00:00:42,916
So the total momentum is
the same before and after.

19
00:00:42,916 --> 00:00:44,363
In every collision.

20
00:00:44,963 --> 00:00:46,990
Kinetic energy is the test.

21
00:00:46,990 --> 00:00:50,671
If it is the same after,
the collision is elastic.

22
00:00:51,071 --> 00:00:54,262
If some kinetic energy
is lost, it is inelastic.

23
00:00:54,262 --> 00:00:58,093
And if the bodies stick together,
it is perfectly inelastic.

24
00:00:58,743 --> 00:01:02,002
Cart A, three kilograms,
at four metres per second.

25
00:01:02,002 --> 00:01:04,128
Cart B, one kilogram, at rest.

26
00:01:04,478 --> 00:01:05,991
Spring bumpers first.

27
00:01:05,991 --> 00:01:08,740
A slows to two, B shoots off at six.

28
00:01:08,740 --> 00:01:12,399
Kinetic energy: twenty four
joules, before and after.

29
00:01:12,399 --> 00:01:13,299
Elastic.

30
00:01:13,525 --> 00:01:15,028
Soft rubber bumpers.

31
00:01:15,028 --> 00:01:18,611
A moves at two point five,
B at four point five.

32
00:01:18,611 --> 00:01:21,627
Kinetic energy falls
to nineteen point five.

33
00:01:21,627 --> 00:01:22,527
Inelastic.

34
00:01:22,879 --> 00:01:24,014
Sticky bumpers.

35
00:01:24,014 --> 00:01:26,721
They lock, and move together at three.

36
00:01:26,721 --> 00:01:28,799
Only eighteen joules are left.

37
00:01:28,799 --> 00:01:30,188
Perfectly inelastic.

38
00:01:30,588 --> 00:01:32,421
But look at the momentum.

39
00:01:32,421 --> 00:01:35,862
Twelve before, and twelve
after, all three times.

40
00:01:36,362 --> 00:01:38,802
Slow-mo replay, at the biggest squash.

41
00:01:39,152 --> 00:01:42,615
For an instant, both move
at three metres per second.

42
00:01:42,615 --> 00:01:44,645
Six joules sit in the bumpers.

43
00:01:44,645 --> 00:01:46,126
Springs give it back.

44
00:01:46,126 --> 00:01:47,713
Clay keeps it as heat.

45
00:01:48,363 --> 00:01:51,288
Now let's write the rules, step by step.

46
00:01:51,638 --> 00:01:52,538
Step one.

47
00:01:52,469 --> 00:01:57,006
No outside push during the hit, so
the total momentum before equals the

48
00:01:57,006 --> 00:01:57,988
total after.

49
00:01:58,738 --> 00:01:59,638
Step two.

50
00:01:59,624 --> 00:02:00,524
Elastic.

51
00:02:00,391 --> 00:02:04,376
The kinetic energy after equals
the kinetic energy before.

52
00:02:05,126 --> 00:02:06,026
Step three.

53
00:02:06,026 --> 00:02:06,926
Inelastic.

54
00:02:06,818 --> 00:02:09,028
The kinetic energy after is less.

55
00:02:09,028 --> 00:02:12,098
The lost part becomes
heat, sound and a dent.

56
00:02:12,848 --> 00:02:13,748
Step four.

57
00:02:13,697 --> 00:02:15,034
Perfectly inelastic.

58
00:02:15,034 --> 00:02:18,487
They share one speed: total
momentum over total mass.

59
00:02:18,487 --> 00:02:21,287
Twelve over four, three metres per second.

60
00:02:22,037 --> 00:02:23,118
Remember this.

61
00:02:23,118 --> 00:02:25,554
Momentum is kept in every collision.

62
00:02:25,554 --> 00:02:28,140
Kinetic energy, only in elastic ones.

63
00:02:28,840 --> 00:02:32,284
Now, the striker and the
clay ball, side by side.

64
00:02:32,634 --> 00:02:34,626
The striker and coin are hard.

65
00:02:34,626 --> 00:02:37,194
They squash a little, then spring back.

66
00:02:37,194 --> 00:02:38,251
Nearly elastic.

67
00:02:38,651 --> 00:02:41,573
The clay squashes, and
does not spring back.

68
00:02:41,573 --> 00:02:43,164
It sticks to the coin.

69
00:02:43,164 --> 00:02:44,485
Perfectly inelastic.

70
00:02:45,085 --> 00:02:46,132
Now you try.

71
00:02:46,132 --> 00:02:50,982
With no outside force, which is always
kept, momentum or kinetic energy?

72
00:02:50,982 --> 00:02:52,283
Pause and decide.

73
00:02:55,483 --> 00:02:56,383
Momentum!

74
00:02:56,184 --> 00:03:00,167
The pushes during the hit are
equal and opposite, so they cancel.

75
00:03:00,167 --> 00:03:01,919
Kinetic energy can be lost.

76
00:03:02,569 --> 00:03:03,469
One more.

77
00:03:03,378 --> 00:03:07,641
Two clay balls, same mass, same
speed, hit head on and stick.

78
00:03:07,641 --> 00:03:09,565
What kinetic energy is left?

79
00:03:09,565 --> 00:03:10,778
Pause and think.

80
00:03:13,978 --> 00:03:14,878
Zero!

81
00:03:14,513 --> 00:03:17,609
The total momentum was
zero, so they stop dead.

82
00:03:17,609 --> 00:03:20,504
All the kinetic energy
becomes heat and sound.

83
00:03:21,154 --> 00:03:23,487
So why does the clay ball not bounce?

84
00:03:23,487 --> 00:03:24,645
Follow the links.

85
00:03:24,995 --> 00:03:25,895
One.

86
00:03:25,479 --> 00:03:27,132
On impact, it squashes.

87
00:03:27,132 --> 00:03:29,773
Its kinetic energy is stored in the dent.

88
00:03:30,173 --> 00:03:31,073
Two.

89
00:03:30,633 --> 00:03:33,052
A spring would push back and return it.

90
00:03:33,052 --> 00:03:34,072
Clay does not.

91
00:03:34,072 --> 00:03:36,066
It stays dented, and warms up.

92
00:03:36,466 --> 00:03:37,366
Three.

93
00:03:36,995 --> 00:03:40,205
With no push back, nothing
drives the two bodies apart.

94
00:03:40,605 --> 00:03:41,505
Four.

95
00:03:41,142 --> 00:03:44,248
So they move off together,
at one shared speed.

96
00:03:44,248 --> 00:03:46,060
That is perfectly inelastic.

97
00:03:46,660 --> 00:03:48,350
Now, one worked example.

98
00:03:48,700 --> 00:03:53,157
A two kilogram ball at six metres
per second hits a four kilogram ball

99
00:03:53,157 --> 00:03:55,614
at rest, and bounces back at two.

100
00:03:55,614 --> 00:03:57,543
Find the other ball's speed.

101
00:03:57,543 --> 00:03:58,741
Elastic or not?

102
00:03:59,241 --> 00:04:01,090
Momentum, twelve before.

103
00:04:01,090 --> 00:04:03,453
After, minus four plus four v.

104
00:04:03,453 --> 00:04:05,826
So v is four metres per second.

105
00:04:06,576 --> 00:04:08,133
Kinetic energy before.

106
00:04:08,133 --> 00:04:10,383
Half times two times thirty six.

107
00:04:10,383 --> 00:04:11,737
Thirty six joules.

108
00:04:12,487 --> 00:04:14,991
After, four joules plus thirty two.

109
00:04:14,991 --> 00:04:16,294
Thirty six again.

110
00:04:16,294 --> 00:04:18,807
Nothing is lost, so it is elastic.

111
00:04:19,457 --> 00:04:20,921
Now, the exam trap.

112
00:04:21,271 --> 00:04:25,790
Many students say that in an inelastic
collision, momentum is lost too.

113
00:04:26,140 --> 00:04:28,966
But momentum is kept in every collision.

114
00:04:28,966 --> 00:04:31,030
Only kinetic energy is lost.

115
00:04:31,680 --> 00:04:33,013
Where is this asked?

116
00:04:33,013 --> 00:04:36,877
In numericals on speeds after a
hit, and the kinetic energy lost.

117
00:04:37,477 --> 00:04:38,675
Exam level now.

118
00:04:38,675 --> 00:04:43,181
A two kilogram block at six metres
per second hits a one kilogram block

119
00:04:43,181 --> 00:04:45,322
coming the other way at three.

120
00:04:45,322 --> 00:04:46,256
They stick.

121
00:04:46,256 --> 00:04:48,286
Find the kinetic energy lost.

122
00:04:48,286 --> 00:04:49,534
Pause and solve.

123
00:04:54,934 --> 00:04:56,371
The answer is B.

124
00:04:56,371 --> 00:04:57,965
Twenty seven joules.

125
00:04:58,465 --> 00:04:59,365
Momentum.

126
00:04:59,187 --> 00:05:01,278
Twelve, minus three, is nine.

127
00:05:01,278 --> 00:05:05,478
Shared by three kilograms, so they
move at three metres per second.

128
00:05:05,978 --> 00:05:07,587
Kinetic energy before.

129
00:05:07,587 --> 00:05:10,069
Thirty six, plus four point five.

130
00:05:10,069 --> 00:05:11,846
Forty point five joules.

131
00:05:12,346 --> 00:05:15,979
After, half times three times
nine, thirteen point five.

132
00:05:15,979 --> 00:05:18,097
The loss is twenty seven joules.

133
00:05:18,697 --> 00:05:20,237
Here is your recap.

134
00:05:20,237 --> 00:05:21,820
Screenshot this card.

135
00:05:25,020 --> 00:05:27,206
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

136
00:05:27,606 --> 00:05:28,506
One.

137
00:05:28,067 --> 00:05:30,292
What is conserved in every collision?

138
00:05:31,192 --> 00:05:32,092
Two.

139
00:05:31,605 --> 00:05:33,944
How do you test if a collision is elastic?

140
00:05:34,844 --> 00:05:35,744
Three.

141
00:05:35,341 --> 00:05:37,436
Where does the lost kinetic energy go?

142
00:05:38,536 --> 00:05:42,713
Next time: one steel ball swings
in, and exactly one flies out.

143
00:05:42,713 --> 00:05:43,613
Why?

144
00:05:43,206 --> 00:05:46,387
We find out in elastic
collision in one dimension.
